  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Signals as Functions",
    "lesson_core_move": "Map each real-valued time instant $t$ to its signal value $x(t)$, using the function viewpoint to interpret continuous-time signal notation before graphs, piecewise definitions, support, symmetry, and basic parameters.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Signals as Functions",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Continuous-Time Signals as Functions",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A continuous-time signal is a function of time. The input is a time instant $t$, and the output is the signal value at that time, written $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The phrase continuous-time means that $t$ can be any real-valued time, not just a whole-number time step. Times such as $0$, $3$, $-1.5$, and $\\frac{1}{2}$ are all possible inputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The function viewpoint is the basic rule to recognize: a time input gets mapped to a signal value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t \\mapsto x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To use a signal rule, choose a time, substitute that time for $t$, and interpret the result as the value of the signal at that instant. For example, if $x(t)=2t+1$, then choosing $t=3$ gives one specific signal value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 2t+1 \\\\\nx(3) &= 2(3)+1 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $x(3)=7$ means that the signal assigns the value $7$ to the time $3$. This same input-output viewpoint is the foundation for reading signal graphs, piecewise rules, support, symmetry, and basic signal parameters later.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Signals as Functions",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Interpreting $x(t)$ as a Time-to-Value Rule",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the statement $x(2)=5$, identify the time input and the signal value.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the function notation $x(2)$. In signal notation, the number inside the parentheses is the time input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\text{time input}) = \\text{signal value at that time}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So in $x(2)=5$, the input time is $2$. The number after the equals sign is the output of the signal at that time, so the signal value is $5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(2)=5 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad 2 \\mapsto 5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The statement means that time $2$ maps to signal value $5$. No computation is needed here; the task is to read the input and output from the notation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Signals as Functions",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding $x(t_0)$ at a Specific Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $x(t)=3t-2$. Find $x(4)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the requested value $x(4)$. This means the time input is $4$, so we use the rule $x(t)=3t-2$ with $t=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $4$ for every $t$ in the signal rule, then simplify.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nx(4) &= 3(4)-2 \\\\\n&= 12-2 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal value at time $4$ is $10$. In time-to-value language, the rule maps $4$ to $10$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4 \\mapsto 10",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Signals as Functions",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Evaluating a Signal at Non-Integer Times",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $x(t)=4t+1$. Find $x\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the requested value $x\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$. The time input is $\\frac{1}{2}$, which is still a valid continuous-time input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same substitution move as before: replace every $t$ in the rule with the entire fractional input $\\frac{1}{2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nx\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1 \\\\\n&= 2+1 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal value at time $\\frac{1}{2}$ is $3$. The non-integer input changes the arithmetic, but it does not change the function viewpoint.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{1}{2} \\mapsto 3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Signals as Functions",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reading a Signal Rule Over All Real Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=t^2-1$, describe what the rule assigns to an arbitrary time $t$, then find $x(-1)$, $x(0)$, and $x(2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the formula $x(t)=t^2-1$. This is not just one value; it is a rule that takes any real time $t$ and returns the signal value $t^2-1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t \\mapsto t^2-1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To find the requested signal values, use the same substitution move for each time input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nx(-1) &= (-1)^2-1 = 0 \\\\\nx(0) &= 0^2-1 = -1 \\\\\nx(2) &= 2^2-1 = 3\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rule assigns $0$ to time $-1$, $-1$ to time $0$, and $3$ to time $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-1 \\mapsto 0, \\quad 0 \\mapsto -1, \\quad 2 \\mapsto 3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "These are individual values from one complete signal rule. The formula $x(t)=t^2-1$ defines the signal over real time, and each substitution reads one time-to-value pair from that rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
